--TEST--
A decoded field's key being absent reports which column it sits in — required, or optional and filled from its default — because required and nullable are separate questions — `rule:core-api/required-optional-and-nullable` and `rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field`
--FILE--
<?nvs
use Core\Json;

// `rule:core-api/required-optional-and-nullable`: three separate questions, and
// this class writes each column once. `$name` has no default and so is
// required. `$note` is `?string` with no default, so it is required *and*
// accepts `null` — the pair that shows the two columns are independent.
// `$age` has a default and so is the only optional field here.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Note {
    public string $name;
    public ?string $note;
    public int $age;

    public function constructor(string $name, ?string $note, int $age = 0) {
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->note = $note;
        $this->age = $age;
    }
}

// Every key present is the case that decodes, whatever column each sits in.
var $full = Core\Json::decodeAs<Note>("{\"name\":\"ada\",\"note\":\"hi\",\"age\":36}");
echo "name=", $full->name, " note=", $full->note ?? "<none>", " age=", $full->age as string, "\n";

// The second column is about the *type*: a `null` under a nullable field's key
// is accepted, and says nothing about whether the key could have been absent.
var $nulled = Core\Json::decodeAs<Note>("{\"name\":\"ada\",\"note\":null,\"age\":36}");
echo "nulled=", $nulled->note ?? "<none>", "\n";

// The first column, asserted on both sides in one report:
// `rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field` accumulates, so one throw
// carries both required keys' absence — and carries nothing for the optional
// key, whose absence was filled rather than reported. `$note` sits with `$name`
// rather than with `$age`, which is the assertion nullability does not decide
// this.
try {
    Core\Json::decodeAs<Note>("{}");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $missing) {
    echo "issues=", Core\Arr::count($missing->issues) as string, "\n";
    echo "  ", $missing->issues["0"]->path, ": ", $missing->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
    echo "  ", $missing->issues["1"]->path, ": ", $missing->issues["1"]->message, "\n";
}

// The other side of that bound: with both required keys written, the optional
// one's absence is no issue at all and the field holds the constant its own
// constructor parameter declares.
var $filled = Core\Json::decodeAs<Note>("{\"name\":\"ada\",\"note\":null}");
echo "filled=", $filled->age as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
name=ada note=hi age=36
nulled=<none>
issues=2
  name: required field missing
  note: required field missing
filled=0
